\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Quantum computing offers substantial advantages over classical computing, and the prospect of such advantages for optimization has motivated a number of works~\cite{FarhiEtAl2025HighGirthMaxCut,
PirnayEtAl2024InPrincipleAdvantage,
GilyenHastingsVazirani2021AdiabaticAdvantage}.
One such approach is decoded quantum interferometry (DQI)~\cite{jordan2025dqi}. It applies to optimal polynomial intersection (OPI), a polynomial fitting problem over finite fields: each evaluation point comes with a set of desired values, and the goal is to find a polynomial of bounded degree whose evaluation is acceptable at as many points as possible. 
DQI is a polynomial-time quantum algorithm and achieves optimization guarantees for OPI in regimes where all known classical algorithms require exponential time. Recent improvements further support OPI as a candidate for practical, classically verifiable quantum advantage~\cite{khattar2025circuits,rosmanis2026}, though establishing this unconditionally remains a major open challenge in complexity theory.

Aside from time, space is another central resource, especially when logical qubits carry substantial fault-tolerant implementation overhead~\cite{FowlerMariantoniMartinisCleland2012}. This motivates our first question:
\begin{center}
\textit{Does DQI yield quantum space advantage, and can we prove it unconditionally?}
\end{center}
Streaming algorithms provide a natural framework for studying space-efficient computation~\cite{LeGall2006, NayakTouchette2017}. Motivated by the need to process massive datasets~\cite{BabcockBabuDatarMotwaniWidom2002, Muthukrishnan2005}, the streaming model presents input elements sequentially and has found many applications, including computing statistics over data streams~\cite{FlajoletMartin1985, AlonMatiasSzegedy1999} and estimating parameters of massive graphs~\cite{BarYossefKumarSivakumar2002, McGregor2014}.

The number of data passes is a third important resource: revisiting the input many times can reduce memory requirements. Indeed, one-pass quantum streaming advantages are known for estimating the
optimum values of Max-DiCut~\cite{kallaugher2024exponential} and Max-2SAT~\cite{wang2026exponential}, but
constant-pass classical algorithms can already match their performances~\cite{saxena2025streaming,fei2026dichotomy}, raising a second question:\footnote{Similar questions have been studied in different settings and for contrived problems \cite{ggjl2025,girish2022quantum}. We focus on more natural problems: OPI and its Hermite generalization.}
\begin{center}
\textit{Can one-pass quantum algorithms outperform many-pass classical algorithms for natural problems?}
\end{center}

We answer both questions affirmatively in one shot.\footnote{Obviously, this is not saying that the results are proved by an LLM in one shot. See \Cref{sec:open-problems} for AI disclosure.}
We adapt DQI to the streaming setting and prove rigorous classical lower bounds for OPI. Let $N$ be the stream length and let $\alpha\in(0,1]$ be the approximation guarantee: larger $\alpha$ is better and $\alpha=100\%$ finds the exact optimum.

\begin{theorem}[Quantum advantage for OPI]\label{thm:headline-opi}
For some constant $c>0$, the following holds for finding an $\alpha$-approximate solution for OPI in the streaming setting.
\begin{itemize}[labelsep=5pt,leftmargin=15pt,topsep=4pt,itemsep=0pt]
\item $\alpha=99.999\%$.
A one-pass $\widetilde O(\sqrt N)$-space $\exp\bigl(\widetilde O(\sqrt N)\bigr)$-time quantum algorithm suffices.
\item $\alpha=93.301\%$. 
A one-pass $\widetilde O(\sqrt N)$-space $\poly(N)$-time quantum algorithm suffices.
\item $\alpha=75.001\%$. 
Provably no $N^c$-pass $N^{1/2+c}$-space classical algorithm exists, even with unlimited time.
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

Our techniques extend to a natural generalization of OPI using Hermite interpolation. 
In this Hermite OPI, each evaluation point has a set of acceptable values and (high-order) derivatives, and the output polynomial tries to match both the values and the derivatives. 
For this problem, we obtain, qualitatively, the strongest possible quantum streaming advantages.

\begin{theorem}[Stronger advantage for Hermite OPI]\label{thm:headline-hopi}
For some constant $c>0$, the following holds to find an $\alpha$-approximate solution for a version of Hermite OPI in the streaming setting.
\begin{itemize}[labelsep=5pt,leftmargin=15pt,topsep=4pt,itemsep=0pt]
\item $\alpha=99.999\%$. 
A one-pass $\polylog(N)$-space $\exp\bigl(\polylog(N)\bigr)$-time quantum algorithm suffices.
\item $\alpha=93.301\%$. 
A one-pass $\polylog(N)$-space $\polylog(N)$-per-entry-time\footnote{This is the processing time per stream entry arrival, including the time to generate the answer at the end.} quantum algorithm suffices.
\item $\alpha=75.001\%$. 
Provably no $N^c$-pass $N^c$-space classical algorithm exists, even with unlimited time.
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

For the rest of the section, we give the formal definition of Hermite OPI, present our formal theorems, and discuss related works.

\subsection{Problem setup}\label{sec:intro-setup}

Throughout, $\log$ denotes the base-two logarithm,
$[n]=\{1,2,\ldots,n\}$ for each positive integer $n$, $\1[E]$ for the indicator of event $E$, $\Fbb_q$ for the field of size $q$, and
$\Fbb_q^\times=\Fbb_q\setminus\{0\}$.
Big-O notations hide absolute constants unless a subscript is used; for example, $O_\kappa(\cdot)$ allows the hidden constant to depend on $\kappa$.
The notation $\widetilde O(\cdot)$ suppresses polylogarithmic factors and $\poly(\cdot)$ hides a fixed polynomial bound.

\paragraph{Hermite interpolation and Hasse derivatives.}
Polynomial interpolation prescribes a polynomial's values at distinct
points. Hermite interpolation also prescribes derivatives.
Over finite fields, these constraints are expressed using Hasse derivatives:
for $f\in\Fbb_q[X]$, its $j$th Hasse derivative $f^{(j)}$ is defined by\footnote{Consider $f(X)=X^3$ over $\Fbb_3$. Ordinary derivatives show that every derivative vanishes, which is not very informative. Hasse derivatives detect the difference as $f(X+T)=X^3+T^3$ and $f^{(3)}(X)=1$.}
\[
 f(X+T)=\sum_{j\ge0}f^{(j)}(X)T^j
 \quad\text{where $T$ is a formal variable.}
\]
Note that $f^{(0)}=f$. For $\ell\ge1$, the length-$\ell$ jet at $a\in\Fbb_q$ records the first $\ell$ Hasse derivatives:
\[
 J_\ell f(a)=\left(f^{(0)}(a),\ldots,f^{(\ell-1)}(a)\right)\in\Fbb_q^\ell.
\]

Similar to polynomial interpolation, any prescribed length-$\ell$ jets at any $n$ distinct points are realized by a unique polynomial of degree less than $\ell n$.
Our Hermite OPI problem is given a set of allowed jets and asks for a polynomial of smaller degree whose jets belong to as many of these sets as possible.

\begin{problem}[Hermite optimal polynomial intersection (HOPI)]\label{prob:hopi}
Let prime $q\ge3$, integer $\ell\ge1$, and $\alpha\in(0,1]$.
Write $n=q-1$, $\Sigma=\Fbb_q^\ell$, and $K=\ell n/2$,
and identify $\Fbb_q^\times$ with $[n]$.
An instance of $(\alpha,q,\ell)$-HOPI consists of
$S_i\subseteq\Sigma$ of size $(|\Sigma|-1)/2$ for each
$i\in[n]$.
The goal is to output a polynomial
$f\in\Fbb_q[X]$ of degree less than $K$ that $\alpha$-approximately maximizes
\[
 \score(f)
 =\abs{\left\{i\in[n]:J_\ell f(i)\in S_i\right\}}.
\]
In other words, $f$ should satisfy $\score(f)\ge\alpha\cdot\score(g)$ for every $g$ of degree less than $K$.
\end{problem}

The $\ell=1$ version of \Cref{prob:hopi} is the OPI problem~\cite{jordan2025dqi} that only concerns polynomial interpolation.
Here is the HOPI problem in the streaming setting.

\begin{problem}[Streaming HOPI]
\label{prob:streaming-hopi}
In the streaming setting, an $(\alpha,q,\ell)$-HOPI instance is given by $N=n\cdot|\Sigma|$ classical entries
\[
 (i,z,\1[z\in S_i])
 \quad\text{for each}\quad
 i\in[n]
 \quad\text{and}\quad 
 z\in\Sigma,
\] 
which arrive one at a time in some order.
A $\npass$-pass streaming algorithm solves $(\alpha,q,\ell)$-HOPI if it reads the stream $\npass$ times in the same order and, with probability at least $0.99$, outputs an $\alpha$-approximate maximizer $f$ at the end.
\end{problem}

We also remark that a number of parameters in \Cref{prob:hopi} and \Cref{prob:streaming-hopi} can be made tunable\footnote{For example, prime powers, fewer evaluation points, different list sizes, degree bounds, and success rates.} and our results generalize; for simplicity, we work with the current setup.
Streaming algorithms and hardness in this paper adopt the following conventions which we will not repeat afterwards.
\begin{itemize}[labelsep=5pt,leftmargin=15pt,topsep=4pt,itemsep=0pt]
\item \textbf{Space complexity} is the maximum number of (qu)bits used at any time in the computation.
\item \textbf{Preprocessing-update-postprocessing time} measures the runtime to prepare before any stream entry, to update per entry arrival, and to produce the answer after the stream, respectively.
\item \textbf{Input instance and streaming order.} 
All our algorithms work for any instance and any streaming order.
All our lower bounds hold for uniformly random instances in a fixed grouped order: each $S_i$ is independently uniform and the entries $(i,z,\1[z\in S_i])$ arrive consecutively as groups of $i$. The group order and order within groups can be arbitrarily fixed in advance.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Our results}\label{sec:intro-results}

Define
$$
\alpha_\star=\frac{2+\sqrt3}{4}\approx0.93301
\quad\text{and}\quad
\kappa(\alpha)=\frac{4\alpha-3}{4\alpha-2}
\quad\text{for every $\alpha\in[3/4,1]$.}
$$
Our first algorithm adapts DQI to streaming HOPI and achieves every fixed approximation ratio below $\alpha_\star$ with triple efficiency: small space, fast updates, and a single pass over the input.
We prove \Cref{thm:quantum-poly} in \Cref{sec:quantum-streaming}.

\begin{restatable}{theorem}{QuantumStreamingUpper}
\label{thm:quantum-poly}
Let $\alpha\in(0,\alpha_\star)$ be arbitrary.
For every prime $q\ge\Omega_\alpha(1)$ and every integer $\ell\ge1$, there is a one-pass quantum streaming algorithm solving $(\alpha,q,\ell)$-HOPI with
$O(q\ell\log q)$ space complexity and $\poly(q,\ell)$ preprocessing-update-postprocessing time.
\end{restatable}

Allowing exponential-time postprocessing lets us push the approximation ratio arbitrarily close to $1$ with the same low space complexity and a single pass.
Preprocessing and update time remain polynomial.
We prove \Cref{thm:quantum-exp-summary} in \Cref{sec:quantum-near-optimal}.

\begin{restatable}{theorem}{QuantumNearOptimalUpper}
\label{thm:quantum-exp-summary}
Let $\alpha\in(0,1)$ and integer $\ell\ge1$ be arbitrary.
For every prime $q\ge\Omega_\alpha(\ell\log(\ell+1))$, there is a one-pass quantum streaming
algorithm solving $(\alpha,q,\ell)$-HOPI with $O(q\ell\log q)$ space
complexity, $\poly(q,\ell)$ preprocessing-update time, and
$q^{O(q\ell)}$ postprocessing time.
\end{restatable}

Classical algorithms require substantially more space or passes to achieve comparable approximation, even with unlimited computation time.
\Cref{thm:classical-lower-summary}, proved in \Cref{sec:classical}, quantifies this tradeoff for every approximation ratio above $3/4$.

\begin{restatable}{theorem}{ClassicalStreamingLower}
\label{thm:classical-lower-summary}
Let $\alpha\in(3/4,1]$, integers $\ell,\npass\ge1$, and prime $q\ge3$ be arbitrary.
Any randomized $\npass$-pass classical streaming algorithm solving $(\alpha,q,\ell)$-HOPI has space complexity at least
\[
 \frac{q^{1+\ell\cdot\kappa(\alpha)}}
 {\npass}\cdot(\ell\log q)^{-C_\alpha\cdot\ell},
\]
where $C_\alpha>0$ is a constant depending only on $\alpha$.
\end{restatable}

Combining this tradeoff with our quantum upper bounds and choosing concrete parameters gives the headline results \Cref{thm:headline-opi,thm:headline-hopi}.
We present these calculations in \Cref{sec:headline_calc}.

Finally, our classical lower bounds are essentially optimal.
\Cref{thm:classical-upper-summary}, proved in \Cref{sec:classical-upper}, gives a one-pass classical algorithm with nearly matching space complexity for approximation ratios above $3/4$.

\begin{restatable}{theorem}{ClassicalStreamingUpper}
\label{thm:classical-upper-summary}
Let $\alpha\in(3/4,1)$, integer $\ell\ge1$, and prime $q\ge3$ be arbitrary. There is a randomized one-pass classical streaming algorithm solving $(\alpha,q,\ell)$-HOPI with $O_{\ell,\alpha}(q^{1+\ell\kappa(\alpha)}\log q)$ space complexity, $\poly(q,\ell)$ preprocessing-update time, and $q^{O(q\ell)}$ postprocessing time.
\end{restatable}

For approximation ratios at most $3/4$, classical algorithms can match the space and time bounds of our efficient one-pass quantum algorithm.
We prove \Cref{thm:classical-three-quarters-summary} in \Cref{sec:c34}.

\begin{restatable}{theorem}{ClassicalThreeQuartersUpper}
\label{thm:classical-three-quarters-summary}
For every sufficiently large prime $q$, every $\alpha\in(0,3/4]$, and every integer $\ell\ge1$, there is a randomized one-pass classical streaming algorithm solving $(\alpha,q,\ell)$-HOPI with $O(q\ell\log q)$ space complexity and $\poly(q,\ell)$ preprocessing-update-postprocessing time.
\end{restatable}

Our results fit into a broader coding-theoretic picture. At a high level, DQI and related quantum advantage proposals come from Regev's reduction~\cite{Reg09} applied to different code families.
In \Cref{tab:code-advantages}, we summarize them based on the codes, the associated problems, and their corresponding quantum advantages.

