\subsection{Related work}\label{sec:related-works}

\paragraph{Quantum algorithms based on Regev's reduction.}
Regev's quantum reduction~\cite{Reg09}, originally used to establish
hardness of Learning with Errors, combines coherent decoding with
Fourier sampling. Chen, Liu, and
Zhandry~\cite{ChenLiuZhandry2022Filtering} turn this framework into
polynomial-time quantum algorithms for certain average-case
$\mathrm{SIS}^{\infty}$ and related constrained-integer-solution
problems. Recently, Kothari, O'Donnell, and Wu~\cite{KothariODonnellWu2025}
give classical algorithms matching or improving these guarantees,
building in part on earlier classical zero-sum algorithms of Imran and
Ivanyos~\cite{ImranIvanyos2024ZeroSum}.
Very recently, Kothari, Metger, O'Donnell, Shutty, and
Wu~\cite{KothariEtAlQuantumSubsetSum} re-establishes a potential exponential
quantum speedup for one of their proposals.
Coding-theoretic adaptations and extensions are developed
in~\cite{DebrisAlazardRemaudTillich2024,ChaillouxTillich2024QuantumDecoding,BlanvillainChaillouxTillich2025}.
Yamakawa and Zhandry~\cite{yamakawaZhandry2024} use Fourier interference and decoding to obtain an exponential quantum query advantage for codeword search relative to random oracles. Coherent decoding and Fourier sampling also underlie DQI and
our streaming implementation.

\paragraph{DQI and OPI.}
Jordan et al.~\cite{jordan2025dqi} introduce DQI for optimization
and obtain OPI guarantees beyond known efficient classical algorithms.
Subsequent work improves decoding circuits~\cite{khattar2025circuits}
and gives a nearly linear-time OPI implementation in a quantum
random-access model~\cite{rosmanis2026}.
Soft-decoding methods improve guarantees in certain average-case
regimes~\cite{chaillouxTillich2025soft,chailloux2025opi},
while recent work gives existential and algorithmic improvements
beyond the DQI semicircle law for worst-case
OPI~\cite{sunWootters2026,horinagaYamakawa2026,jo2026sampling}. Broader extensions of DQI are studied in~\cite{GuJordan2025Hermitian,bu2026weighted,KrajenbrinkEtAl2026BeyondHamming,SchmidhuberEtAl2025HamiltonianDQI,BuGuLi2026GeneralPauli}, and its computational power and limitations
in~\cite{marwaha2025complexity,parekh2025maxcut,anschuetz2026spin,KramerSchubertEisert2026BoundedDegree}. List decoding already appears in quantum reductions~\cite{chaillouxTillich2025soft,horinagaYamakawa2026,jo2026sampling}.
For fixed $\ell$, our near-optimal algorithm combines sampled-coordinate isolation with exhaustive reversible decoding to implement coherent syndrome compression within the streaming space bound, without further input access; see \Cref{sec:quantum-near-optimal}. Our results establish unconditional space--pass advantages for OPI and Hermite OPI.
\paragraph{Quantum streaming.}
Early quantum space separations were obtained by Le Gall ~\cite{LeGall2006} and for Boolean Hidden Matching~\cite{GavinskyKempeKerenidisRazDeWolf2008}. Subsequent work studied natural problems, including $\mathrm{Dyck}(2)$ and triangle counting~\cite{JainNayak2014,NayakTouchette2017,kallaugher2022quantum}.  Multipass quantum tradeoffs have also been studied for frequency-moment estimation~\cite{Montanaro2016, HamoudiMagniez2019} and Shannon entropy estimation~\cite{FengEtAl2026}. 
For optimization, Kallaugher, Parekh, and
Voronova~\cite{kallaugher2024exponential} and Wang and Yang~\cite{wang2026exponential} obtain one-pass quantum space
advantages for estimating the optimum values of Max-DiCut and
Max-$k$SAT, respectively.
Constant-pass classical algorithms already surpass the corresponding
quantum approximation guarantees for Max-DiCut and Max-$k$SAT~\cite{fei2026dichotomy}.
Our algorithms output an explicit polynomial, and our lower bounds
persist against many-pass classical algorithms, even with unlimited
computation. The quantum algorithm still uses one pass.

\paragraph{Communication and coding techniques.}
G{\"o}{\"o}s et al.~\cite{ggjl2025} obtain an exponential separation
between quantum simultaneous messages and unrestricted randomized
interaction for a total, classically verifiable search relation,
providing a direct antecedent to our work.
We observe that the Forrelation-based construction
of~\cite{girish2022quantum} also yields a streaming space--pass
separation for indexed-bit inputs.
Our classical lower bound uses structure-versus-randomness
and gadgetless-lifting
techniques~\cite{gpw2020,yangzhang2024,maoyangzhang2024,ggjl2025},
including multiparty min-entropy methods~\cite{huang2025minentropy}.
Our Hermite list-recovery bound extends hidden-derivative
interpolation~\cite{bcpzz2026,jeronimo2026} to jet constraints,
using total-incidence accounting~\cite{jeronimo2026} and
Kopparty's differential-root bound~\cite[Theorem~4.3]{kopparty2015}.
Together with multiplicity-code list
decoding~\cite{KoppartyRonZewiSarafWootters2023}, this yields
the list bound for our near-optimal quantum algorithm.
Sampled-coordinate isolation has a classical antecedent in
the \texttt{PRUNE} procedure
of~\cite[Section~3.1]{KoppartyRonZewiSarafWootters2023}.

\paragraph{Other quantum optimization algorithms.}
Quantum optimization advantages have been studied under computational assumptions~\cite{PirnayEtAl2024InPrincipleAdvantage,Szegedy2022QuantumAdvantageSimplified} and in oracle models~\cite{GilyenHastingsVazirani2021AdiabaticAdvantage,LengEtAl2025OptimizationSpeedup}. Related work studies nonconvex
optimization~\cite{LengZhengWu2023NonconvexSeparation} and constant-depth QAOA for Max-Cut on high-girth 3-regular graphs~\cite{FarhiEtAl2025HighGirthMaxCut}. These works concern time, query complexity, or solution quality.
Our results establish unconditional streaming space--pass separations
for explicitly presented optimization instances, even with unlimited classical computation.

\subsection{Open problems and AI disclosure}
\label{sec:open-problems}

Our quantum upper bounds leave open the cost of obtaining stronger
approximation guarantees.
\Cref{thm:quantum-poly} achieves every approximation ratio below
$\alpha_\star$ with one pass, $O(q\ell\log q)$ space, and polynomial
preprocessing, update, and postprocessing time.
For fixed $\ell$, the near-optimal guarantee in
\Cref{thm:quantum-exp-summary} reaches every ratio below $1$,
but uses exponential-time postprocessing.
Can polynomial-time postprocessing suffice with similar space and
pass bounds?
Alternatively, can one establish computational hardness?
Exact optimization raises a separate question: does finding an optimal
polynomial require more quantum memory, or can a one-pass algorithm
achieve it in small space with unrestricted postprocessing?

On the classical side, the one-pass bounds already match up to low-order terms. The remaining tradeoffs concern additional passes and
computational efficiency.
Can algorithms match the inverse dependence on the number of passes
in \Cref{thm:classical-lower-summary}, particularly when their memory
is comparable to the quantum budget?
Can they achieve the approximation--space frontier without
exponential-time postprocessing?

A broader question is which other code families support quantum
streaming advantages through Regev's reduction.
Can we obtain a coding-theoretic classification? A first step would be completing the missing entries in \Cref{tab:code-advantages}.

\paragraph{AI disclosure.}
The authors formulated the questions in \Cref{sec:introduction} and observed the streaming DQI algorithm (\Cref{thm:quantum-poly}); GPT-6 Astra was used to check and refine the proof. 
The authors subsequently proposed the main idea for \Cref{thm:classical-lower-summary}, whose overall proof builds on the framework developed by one of the authors \cite{yangzhang2024} before the LLM age.  GPT-6 Astra was used to help improve and extend bounds from \cite{bcpzz2026,jeronimo2026} to prove \Cref{hendpoint:lr}. After completing \Cref{thm:quantum-poly} and \Cref{thm:classical-lower-summary}, the authors aimed to complete the tradeoff curve and prompted GPT-6 Astra for classical upper bounds, leading to \Cref{thm:classical-upper-summary} and \Cref{thm:classical-three-quarters-summary}. \Cref{thm:quantum-exp-summary} was the last derived: the authors observed that the streaming DQI algorithm is a soft state identification problem whose information-theoretic answer should be better than what efficient unique decoding can provide; guided by this, GPT-6 Astra completed the proof.

GPT-6 was also used to draft the paper, which was fully rewritten by the authors.
The authors take full responsibility for the correctness, exposition, and attribution in the final manuscript.

