\section{Technical overview}\label{sec:overview}

We sketch the main ideas behind our quantum algorithms and classical bounds.
For convenience, let $n=q-1$ and we identify $\Fbb_q^\times$ by $[n]$.
Set $\Sigma=\Fbb_q^\ell$ and $K=\ell n/2$.
The Hermite code\footnote{It is the univariate multiplicity code in \cite{kopparty2015}. We call it Hermite code to be consistent with Hermite OPI.} $C_\Hrm\subseteq\Sigma^n$ consists of the jet vectors
$(J_\ell f(i))_{i\in[n]}$ of polynomials $f\in\Fbb_q[X]$ with $\deg f<K$.
Let $G_\Hrm$ be its generator matrix such that $G_\Hrm^\top u$ is the jets of
$f_u(X)=\sum_{r=0}^{K-1}u_rX^r$.
The goal of HOPI can be equivalently phrased as finding $u\in\Fbb_q^K$ with large 
$$
\score(u):=\score(f_u)=\sum_{i\in[n]}\1[(G_\Hrm^\top u)_i\in S_i],
$$
given allowed jets $S_i\subseteq\Sigma$ for each $i\in[n]$.

\subsection{Triply efficient quantum streaming at \texorpdfstring{$93\%$}{93\%} approximation}\label{subsec:tri}

We adapt decoded quantum interferometry
(DQI)~\cite{jordan2025dqi} to the one-pass streaming model.
The algorithm loads the streaming input into a quantum state, after which it projects onto errors that admit unique syndrome decoding and uses Fourier interference to sample a high-scoring polynomial. 
All stages use small space and are also efficient in time. 
\Cref{fig:streaming-hermite-dqi} depicts the full algorithm.

\input{figures/figure_streaming_dqi}

Fix $\alpha$ near $\alpha_\star\approx0.93301$ and choose
$\lambda\in(0,1/4)$ such that $\frac12+\sqrt{\lambda(1-\lambda)}=\alpha$.
The choice of $\lambda$ leaves the typical error weight a constant
fraction below the decoding cutoff near $n/4$, while placing the
typical score around $\alpha n$. 
Also for intuition, we assume each $|S_i|=|\Sigma|/2$, which only hides a $1/|\Sigma|$ deviation that goes to zero as $q$ grows.

\paragraph{One-pass state preparation
(\Cref{fig:streaming-hermite-dqi}a and the top of b).}
Our target product state follows the binomial-weight DQI construction in \cite{rosmanis2026}. Define for each $i\in[n]$
\[
 \ket{h_i}=|\Sigma|^{-1/2}\sum_{z\in\Sigma}(-1)^{\1[z\notin S_i]}\ket{z}.
\]
Since we assumed $|S_i|=|\Sigma|/2$ for intuition, $\ket{h_i}$ is a unit vector orthogonal to the uniform state $\ket{\phi_0}=|\Sigma|^{-1/2}\sum_{z\in\Sigma}\ket{z}$.
Define $\ket{\widehat h_i}=\Fsf_\Sigma\ket{h_i}$, where $\Fsf_\Sigma=\Fsf_q^{\otimes\ell}$ is the Fourier transform.
Our goal is to prepare the product error state
\[
 \ket{\Psi}
 =\bigotimes_{i=1}^n
   \left(\sqrt{1-\lambda}\ket{0}
         +\sqrt\lambda\ket{\widehat h_i}\right).
\]
To see why this can be done with one-pass streaming, we initialize an address register to $\ket{\phi_0}$
and a control qubit to
$\sqrt{1-\lambda}\ket{0}+\sqrt\lambda\ket{1}$ for each $i\in[n]$.
Then each stream entry $(i,z,0)$ indicates $z\notin S_i$ and we flip the phase of
$\ket{1}\ket{z}$. After the pass, applying $\Fsf_\Sigma$ to the address register gives $\sqrt{1-\lambda}\ket{0}\ket{0}
 +\sqrt\lambda\ket{1}\ket{\widehat h_i}$.
Since $\braket{0|\widehat h_i}=0$, a controlled-flip produces the desired $\ket\Psi$.

\paragraph{Computing the syndrome and erasing the error
(yellow arrows in \Cref{fig:streaming-hermite-dqi}b).}
Before decoding, set $d\approx n/4$ and project onto errors
of weight at most $d$. Write
\[
 \Pi_d=\sum_{\wt(e)\le d}\ket{e}\bra{e},
 \qquad
 \ket{\Phi}:=\Pi_d\ket{\Psi}
 =\sum_{\wt(e)\le d}a_e\ket{e}.
\]
We measure only the projection's acceptance flag, preserving coherence
among the remaining errors. On acceptance, the state is
$\ket{\Phi}/\|\Phi\|$. Then, following DQI~\cite{jordan2025dqi}, we compute the syndrome and
coherently erase the error register:
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \frac1{\|\Phi\|}\sum_{\wt(e)\le d}a_e\ket{e}\ket{0}
 &\longmapsto
 \frac1{\|\Phi\|}\sum_{\wt(e)\le d}
   a_e\ket{e}\ket{G_\Hrm e}
 \longmapsto
 \ket{0}\otimes\frac1{\|\Phi\|}
   \sum_{\wt(e)\le d}a_e\ket{G_\Hrm e}.
 \end{aligned}
\]
The erasure is possible by efficient unique decoding (\Cref{thm:hermite-compact-decoder}).

\paragraph{Interference and sampling
(bottom of \Cref{fig:streaming-hermite-dqi}b).}
Applying $(\Fsf_q^{-1})^{\otimes K}$ to the syndrome register
makes the Fourier terms interfere. Let $V$ denote the map that
converts $\ket{e}$ to $\ket{G_\Hrm e}$ and then applies
this inverse Fourier transform. With
$\omega=e^{2\pi\mathrm i/q}$, it satisfies
\[
 V\ket{e}=q^{-K/2}
 \sum_{u\in\Fbb_q^K}\omega^{\langle G_\Hrm^\top u,e\rangle}\ket{u}.
\]
On the projected errors, syndrome compression is an isometry.
Fourier inversion shows that the amplitude depends
on $u$ through a polynomial $P$ of
$\score(u)=\sum_i\1[(G_\Hrm^\top u)_i\in S_i]$ with $\deg P\le d$.
The normalized output state is
\[
 \ket{\Dcal_P}
 =\frac{V\ket{\Phi}}{\|\Phi\|}
 =\frac{q^{-K/2}}{\|\Phi\|}
   \sum_{u\in\Fbb_q^K}P(\score(u))\ket{u}.
\]
Measuring the output register samples $f_u$ with probability
proportional to $|P(\score(u))|^2$.

\paragraph{Why the sampled polynomial is a good approximation.}
The score bias comes from the inverse transform of the product state.
Write $\Fsf_\Hrm=\Fsf_\Sigma^{\otimes n}$.
Measuring $\Fsf_\Hrm^{-1}\ket{\Psi}$ gives independent row
assignments, which need not be the jets of a single polynomial.
Each row satisfies its constraint with probability
\[
\left(\sqrt{\lambda/2}
          +\sqrt{(1-\lambda)/2}\right)^2=\alpha.
\]
Hence their total score concentrates around $\alpha n$.
Since the truncation happens at $d\approx n/4\ge\lambda n$, $\ket\Phi$ is close to $\ket\Psi$. This intuitively shows that the concentration from $\ket\Psi$ transfers to $\ket\Phi$. To make this rigorous, a second-moment calculation similar to \cite{jordan2025dqi} is needed.

Finally to see why $\alpha_\star$ is the right threshold, the decoding cutoff allows any fixed $\lambda<1/4$, and
\[
 \alpha=\frac12+\sqrt{\lambda(1-\lambda)}
 \longrightarrow\alpha_\star=\frac12+\frac{\sqrt3}{4}\approx0.93301
 \qquad\text{as }\lambda\to\frac14.
\]
Hence every fixed approximation ratio $\alpha<\alpha_\star$ is
achievable by choosing $\lambda$ sufficiently close to $1/4$.

\subsection{Doubly efficient quantum streaming at \texorpdfstring{$1-\epsilon$}{1-epsilon} approximation}

Following \Cref{subsec:tri}, we still prepare in one pass the product state
\[
 \ket{\Psi}
 =\bigotimes_{i=1}^n
   \left(\sqrt{1-\lambda}\ket{0}
         +\sqrt\lambda\ket{\widehat h_i}\right).
\]
For approximation ratio $\alpha$ approaching $1$, we need to choose $\lambda\approx1/2$ such that $\frac12+\sqrt{\lambda(1-\lambda)}=\alpha$. Set $d\approx\lambda n$ as the projection cutoff.
The Fourier weight remains concentrated below $d$, and the
$\ket\Psi$ score is still concentrated around $\alpha\cdot n$.
The difficulty is that errors in this larger weight range may
share a syndrome, preventing unique decoding.

\paragraph{Resolving ambiguity with a few coordinates.}
Write $B_t=\{e\in\Sigma^n:\wt(e)\le t\}$.
Our list-decoding analysis bounds every syndrome list in
$B_{d+2}$ by $2^{o(n)}$ (\Cref{lem:nearopt-dual-lists}).
Let $r=o(n)$. Choose a uniform $r$-subset $R\subseteq[n]$ and append the error blocks on $R$:
\[
 H_Re=(G_\Hrm e,e_R),
 \qquad
 H_R=\begin{pmatrix}G_\Hrm\\E_R\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Two distinct errors with the same original syndrome differ on
more than half the blocks, since their difference is a nonzero
dual codeword.
Consequently, for fixed $e\in B_d$, the probability that
some competitor in $B_{d+2}$ also agrees with $e$ on $R$ is at most
$2^{o(n)}\cdot2^{-r}$.
Taking $r=o(n)$ makes this probability negligible.

We accept only errors in $B_d$ that are uniquely identified by their augmented syndrome within $B_{d+2}$. Averaging over $R$, this filter discards only small probability mass.
To decode $\ket e$ from $\ket{H_Re}$ for accepted errors $e$, we do not have an efficient algorithm and  have to use exhaustive search to enumerate all possible $e$'s. This is time inefficient but the space bound remains the same.

\paragraph{Extra coordinates cost little.}
After inverse Fourier transformation, each $(u,v)$
corresponds to
\[
 H_R^\top(u,v)
 =G_\Hrm^\top u+E_R^\top v.
\]
The additional term changes only the coordinates in $R$.
Thus, writing $S(z)=\sum_i\1[z_i\in S_i]$, we can discard
$v$ and lose at most $r$ constraints. Since $r=o(n)$, we have
\[
 \score(u)\ge S\left(H_R^\top(u,v)\right)-r\approx S(H_R^\top(u,v)).
\]
The sampled coordinates therefore resolve decoding ambiguity
and ensure the final score is roughly $\alpha\cdot n$.

\subsection{Classical lower bounds}

We prove the streaming lower bound using multi-party communication complexity. This turns out to be a challenging task. First, most of the current communication lower bound techniques only work for boolean functions (or relations with few solutions), but HOPI has exponentially many solutions for a typical random input. 
To handle this, we use the gadgetless lifting framework developed by \cite{yangzhang2024,maoyangzhang2024}. 
Furthermore, in the gadgetless lifting framework, the choice of the error-correcting code $C_\Hrm$ for HOPI is a Hermite code, whose coding-theoretic properties (in particular, list recovery bounds) are not well established; and we extend the hidden-derivative interpolation of~\cite{bcpzz2026,jeronimo2026} for the analysis.

Below, we give an overview of our proof with presentation adapted from~\cite{ggjl2025}. In the $k$-party communication problem, we partition complete lists $S_1,...,S_n$ among $k$ parties, each holding $n/k$ input lists. For intuition, first consider a simplified input distribution in which the membership bits $\1[z\in S_i]$, indexed by $(i,z)\in[n]\times\Sigma$, are independent and uniform. For $S=(S_1,\ldots,S_n)$ and $c\in C_\Hrm$, write $\err_S(c)=|\{i\in[n]:c_i\notin S_i\}|$. We sketch a lower bound for finding a codeword $c$ with $\err_S(c)\le(1-\alpha)n$. This completes the proof as it is easy to show that the optimum is $n$ for a random $S$ (\Cref{app:classical-random-sat}).

For a communication protocol succeeding with probability at least $2/3$, averaging lets us fix its random coins while maintaining success probability at
least $1/2$ over the input distribution. We require the protocol to announce its output and the membership bits queried by that output, and to return $\bot$ if its score is below $\alpha n$. This verification costs $O_\ell(n\log n)$ additional bits and is performed before the protocol refinement below.
Recall that the stream consists of $N$ entries $(i,z,\1[z\in S_i])$.
For $X\subseteq\{0,1\}^N$ and $I\subseteq[N]$, we use $X_I:=\{x_I\in\{0,1\}^I:x\in X\}$ to denote the set of strings in $X$ restricted to the coordinates in $I$. 

\paragraph{Special case: subcube protocols.}
We start with highly structured protocols. 
For each $a\in[k]$, let $N_a\subset[N]$ be the set of stream bits held by party $a$.
A rectangle $R=X_1\times\cdots\times X_k$, where
$X_a\subseteq\{0,1\}^{N_a}$, is a subcube rectangle if each $X_a$ fixes some coordinates $I_a\subseteq N_a$ and leaves all other coordinates unrestricted. Its codimension is
$\codim(R)=\sum_a|I_a|$.
A subcube protocol is such that every complete-message node of the protocol tree corresponds to a subcube rectangle. This model is closely related to decision trees, but is strictly more general. The following property of the code $C_\Hrm$ will be central to the lower bound.

\begin{definition}[List recovery]
\label{cl:overview-lr-definition}
For $\zeta\in(1/2,1)$ and $T>n$, the code $C_\Hrm\subseteq\Sigma^n$ is
$(\zeta,T,L)$-list-recoverable if,
for every collection $F_1,\ldots,F_n\subseteq\Sigma$ with
$\sum_i|F_i|\le T$,
$$
 \abs{
 \left\{
 x\in C_\Hrm:
 |\{i\in[n]:x_i\in F_i\}|\ge\lceil\zeta n\rceil
 \right\}
 }
 \le L.
$$
\end{definition}

Consider a  deterministic subcube protocol $\Pi$ of communication cost $|\Pi|$, run on uniform input distribution. For a rectangle $R$, Let $F_i(R)$ be the set of all $z\in\Sigma$ for which the membership bit $\1[z\in S_i]$ is fixed on every input in $R$. Then
$\sum_i|F_i(R)|=\codim(R)$.
Thus list recovery applies as long as
$\codim(R)\le T$.

The communication bound $|\Pi|$ controls the probability of exceeding this budget $T$. Let $R_{\mathrm{leaf}}$ be the random leaf reached by
$\Pi$. Under uniform input distribution, a leaf $R$ has probability $2^{-\codim(R)}$, and hence $\E[\codim(R_{\mathrm{leaf}})]=\ent(R_{\mathrm{leaf}})\le|\Pi|$, where $\ent$ denotes binary Shannon entropy. Since the exposed sets only grow along an execution, the exposure budget is exceeded if and only if $\codim(R_{\mathrm{leaf}})>T$. By Markov's inequality,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:overview-codim}
 \Pr[\codim(R_{\mathrm{leaf}})>T]
 \le \frac{\E[\codim(R_{\mathrm{leaf}})]}{T}
 \le \frac{|\Pi|}{T}.
\end{equation}

We analyze each execution up to, but excluding, its first node that exceeds the budget $T$. A codeword $c\in C_\Hrm$ is \emph{dangerous} at $R$
if at least $\lceil\zeta n\rceil$ of its
queried addresses $(i,c_i)$ have their membership bits fixed by $R$. We use two key facts.
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Few dangerous codewords.}
Fix an execution and let $R_*$ be its last rectangle before the budget $T$ is exceeded, or its leaf if the budget is never exceeded.
Since the exposed sets only grow, every codeword that becomes dangerous up to this point remains dangerous at $R_*$. Moreover, $\sum_i|F_i(R_*)|\le T$. \Cref{cl:overview-lr-definition} therefore implies that at most $L$ distinct codewords become dangerous along the entire
execution before the budget is exceeded.

\item \emph{A newly dangerous codeword is unlikely to be a solution.}
Suppose a message first makes a codeword $c$ dangerous, and let $R$ be the rectangle reached after this message, still within the budget.
Immediately before the message, fewer than $\lceil\zeta n\rceil$ of the membership bits queried by $c$ were fixed. The message changes only the sender's factor. Since the sender holds $n/k$ input lists, at most $n/k$ of the bits queried by $c$ belong to the sender. Hence at least 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:overview-tau}
\tau:=n-\frac nk-\lceil\zeta n\rceil\approx\left(1-\frac1k-\zeta\right)n
\end{equation}
queried membership bits in the other parties' inputs remain unfixed at $R$. Conditional on reaching $R$, these bits are independent and uniform.
Fix any $\tau$ of them. The number of zeros among them has distribution $\operatorname{Bin}(\tau,1/2)$, and each zero contributes one to
$\err_S(c)$. Therefore
\begin{equation}\label{eq:overview-eta}
 \Pr[\err_S(c)\le(1-\alpha)n\mid R]
 \le\eta
 \quad\text{where}\quad
 \eta
 :=\Pr[\operatorname{Bin}(\tau,1/2)\le(1-\alpha)n].
\end{equation}
\end{itemize}

Along each execution, order the codewords that become dangerous, before the budget exceeded, by their first appearance.
For each $j\in[L]$, let $E_j$ be the event that the $j$th codeword in the list recovery bound exists and
has $\err_S$ value at most $(1-\alpha)n$.
By the law of total expectation and \eqref{eq:overview-eta}, we have $\Pr[E_j]\le\eta$.
A union bound therefore gives $\Pr\!\left[\exists j\in[L],~E_j\right] \le L\eta$. In words, the probability that any solution codeword becoming dangerous before exceeding the budget is at most $L\eta$.

At a successful leaf, verification exposes every query of the output codeword. Thus, if the budget remains, its successful output is among the dangerous candidates. Combining this with \eqref{eq:overview-codim}, we obtain
\begin{equation}
 \Pr[\Pi\text{ succeeds}]
 \le \frac{|\Pi|}{T}+L\eta.
 \label{cl:overview-subcube-tradeoff}
\end{equation}
Thus, constant success probability and $L\eta=o(1)$ would imply $|\Pi|=\Omega(T)$.

\paragraph{Structure-vs-randomness dichotomy.}
To prove a lower bound for general protocols, we use the
structure-vs-randomness framework developed by Yang and
Zhang \cite{yangzhang2024}, who build upon query-to-communication lifting techniques~\cite{gpw2020}.
We use this framework to refine any general communication protocol
into a subcube-like execution. The nodes of a subcube-like execution
correspond to subcube-like rectangles
$R=X_1\times\cdots\times X_k$ defined such that
\begin{itemize}[itemsep=0pt,topsep=4pt]
 \item $(X_a)_{I_a}$ are fixed strings for some index sets
 $I_a\subseteq N_a$. \hfill (Structure)

 \item $(X_a)_{N_a\setminus I_a}$ are pseudorandom (they ``look'' like
 the uniform distribution). \hfill (Randomness)

 More precisely, they are $\gamma$-dense:
every assignment to any $J\subseteq N_a\setminus I_a$ has mass at most $2^{-\gamma|J|}$. Hence the probability that the $\tau$ unfixed nonsender queries have at most $(1-\alpha)n$ failures is at
most 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:overview-eps-gamma}
\epsilon_\gamma=2^{-\gamma\tau}\binom{\tau}{\le(1-\alpha)n}=2^{(1-\gamma)\tau}\Pr[\operatorname{Bin}(\tau,1/2)\le(1-\alpha)n]=2^{(1-\gamma)\tau}\eta.
\end{equation}
\end{itemize}

This shows that subcube-like protocols behave similarly enough to subcube protocols that we may simply re-do the proof sketched above for subcube protocols, replacing $\eta$ by $\epsilon_\gamma$.
We will also prove that the refinement to obtain the subcube-like protocols incur an $O(1/(1-\gamma))$ blowup in expectation, independently of the number of parties (\Cref{cl:preprocessing}).
By \eqref{eq:overview-tau}, \eqref{eq:overview-eps-gamma}, and \eqref{eq:overview-eta}, we therefore obtain
\begin{equation}\label{eq:after-refine}
 \Pr[\Pi\text{ succeeds}]
 \le
 O\left(\frac{|\Pi|}{(1-\gamma)T}\right)
 +2^{(1-\gamma)\tau}\cdot L\cdot\Pr[\operatorname{Bin}(\tau,1/2)\le(1-\alpha)n],
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}\label{eq:overview-tau-2}
\tau\approx\left(1-\frac1k-\zeta\right)n.
\end{equation}

\paragraph{List recovery of the Hermite code.}
We have been ignoring a non-trivial issue---the list recovery bound required by our argument must be proved for the Hermite code $C_\Hrm$.
We obtain it by extending the hidden-derivative interpolation of~\cite{bcpzz2026,jeronimo2026}. The change is that each stream entry now specifies a length-$\ell$ jet, rather than one polynomial value.
We impose these jet constraints on an auxiliary polynomial $Q(X,Y_0,\ldots,Y_r)$, so that every high-score candidate $P$ satisfies $Q(X,P,P^{(1)},\ldots,P^{(r)})=0$. The differential-root bound of~\cite[Theorem~4.3]{kopparty2015} then bounds the number of candidates. 
For \Cref{cl:overview-lr-definition}, our Hermite extension (\Cref{hendpoint:lr}) gives
\begin{equation}\label{eq:overview-lr}
\zeta=2\alpha-1-\Theta\left(\frac1{\log q}\right),
\qquad
 T=
 \widetilde{\Omega}_{\ell,\alpha}
 \left(q^{1+\ell\cdot\kappa(\alpha)} \right),
 \qquad
 L=2^{o_{\ell,\alpha}(n)},
\end{equation}
where $\kappa(\alpha)=(4\alpha-3)/(4\alpha-2)$.

Our discussion so far has been about a classical communication lower bound. To obtain a streaming lower bound, take $k=\Theta(\log q)$ parties, which ensures $\tau>2\cdot(1-\alpha)n$ by \eqref{eq:overview-tau-2}.
Then choosing $\gamma=1-o_{\ell,\alpha}(1)$ appropriately ensures the second term in \eqref{eq:after-refine} is $L\cdot2^{-o_{\ell,\alpha}(n)}=o(1)$ by \eqref{eq:overview-lr}.
Since the verified protocol succeeds with probability at least $1/2$, \eqref{eq:after-refine} requires $|\Pi|=\Omega((1-\gamma)\cdot T)=\widetilde\Omega(T)$ bits. Finally, a $\npass$-pass streaming algorithm using $S$ bits gives a protocol with $(k\npass-1)S$ communicated bits. 
Thus $S=\widetilde{\Omega}_{\ell,\alpha}\left( q^{1+\ell\cdot\kappa(\alpha)}/\npass\right)$ as claimed.

\subsection{Classical upper bounds}

Finally we give a sketch of our classical streaming algorithms.

Our algorithm for \Cref{thm:classical-upper-summary}  independently keeps each positive address $(i,z,1)$ with rate $\eta$ during a single pass over the stream. After the pass, it exhaustively searches for a polynomial matching at least the acceptance threshold $h_0$ sampled ones.  
The analysis has two parts: showing that every accepted polynomial has high score, and showing that some polynomial is accepted. For the first, Hermite list recovery bounds the number of polynomials at each score. Combining this bound with the sampling rate shows that, with high probability, no polynomial of score below $\alpha n$ matches enough sampled ones.  For the second, we consider sets of $h$ positive addresses belonging to high-score polynomials, which we call certificates. There are many such certificates, and a second-moment argument controls their overlap to show that a complete certificate survives with a small but sufficient probability. A concentration inequality then shows that an almost complete certificate survives with high probability. We therefore set the acceptance threshold $h_0$ slightly below $h$. Together, these two claims ensure that the algorithm returns a polynomial of score at least $\alpha n\ge\alpha\OPT$.  Balancing the sampling rate $\eta$ and threshold $h_0$ yields an $\alpha$-approximation claimed in \Cref{thm:classical-upper-summary}.

Our algorithm for \Cref{thm:classical-three-quarters-summary} combines interpolation with random completion in a single pass. First, it collects one allowed jet at each of $k=n/2-4$ distinct evaluation points. Whenever a positive entry $(i,z,1)$ arrives at a point $i$ not yet selected, it selects $i$ and records $y_i=z\in S_i$. Once $k$ points have been selected, it stops collecting constraints; let $I$ be the set of selected points.
Next, it constructs a constant number $R$ of independent random candidate polynomials of degree less than $K$, each satisfying $J_\ell P(i)=y_i$ for $i\in I$. Hermite interpolation imposes $\ell k$ linear constraints on the $K$ coefficients, leaving $K-\ell k=4\ell$ free parameters. Sampling these parameters uniformly gives a uniformly random polynomial satisfying all the selected constraints.
The candidates can now be scored exactly using the rest of the stream. Each candidate already satisfies the $k$ selected constraints. For each remaining positive entry $(i,z,1)$ with $i\notin I$, the algorithm scores if $J_\ell P(i)=z$. The algorithm returns the candidate with the largest counter.
At each remaining point $i$, a random completion has a uniform jet in $\Sigma$, which belongs to $S_i$ with probability roughly $1/2$. The expected score is therefore roughly $k+n/4=3n/4$.

