\section{Classical streaming lower bounds}
\label{sec:classical}
In this section, we prove the classical streaming lower bound for
$(\alpha,q,\ell)$-HOPI.

\ClassicalStreamingLower*

Fix $\ell$ and $\alpha$ as above. We prove the streaming lower bound by using the $k$-party communication complexity. Index the rows in their fixed group order, write $F_i=S_{a_i}$, and partition them into $k$ consecutive blocks of size at most $\lceil n/k\rceil$, one for each party. We use public-blackboard communication and write $|\Pi|$ for the worst-case communication cost. The communication bound holds even with success probability $2/3$, and therefore applies to the $0.99$ success requirement in \Cref{prob:streaming-hopi}.

Throughout this section, put $h=\ln n$,$\zeta=2\alpha-1-\frac{10}{\ln q}$ and $k=\lceil h\rceil$.  For $F=(F_1,\ldots,F_n)$ and $c\in\Sigma^n$, write $\err_F(c)=|\{i\in[n]:c_i\notin F_i\}|$. 

Recall that the Hermite code is $C_\Hrm=
\left\{\bigl(J_{\ell}P(a_1),\ldots,J_{\ell}P(a_n)\bigr):
 P\in\Pcal_K \right\}\subseteq\Sigma^n$
where $\Sigma=\Fbb_q^\ell$ and $K=\ell n/2$. This code is fixed independently of the input lists.
Using the list-recovery bound in \Cref{hendpoint:lr}, it remains
to prove the following communication lower bound.
\begin{lemma}
\label{cl:communication}
Suppose $C_\Hrm$ is $(\zeta,T,L)$-list-recoverable, where
$T\ge2n$ and $\log L\le A n/h^3$ for some $A>0$.
For $q\ge\Omega_{\alpha,A}(1)$, there is an input distribution such that any communication protocol $\Pi$ giving an $\alpha$-approximation with probability at least $2/3$ over the distribution has
\begin{equation}
 |\Pi|\ge \frac{c\cdot T}{h^2}-\frac{\ell n\log q}c,
 \label{cl:communication-interface}
\end{equation}
where $c>0$ is an absolute constant.
\end{lemma}

We prove the communication lower bound by using the gadgetless lifting idea of \cite{yangzhang2024,maoyangzhang2024} and present the proof by following~\cite[Section~5]{ggjl2025}. We first (\Cref{subsec:classical-subcube}) prove the lower bound for highly structured communication protocols that we call subcube protocols. We then (\Cref{subsec:classical-dense}) generalize
that proof to subcube-like protocols. Finally
(\Cref{cl:preprocessing}), we use the structure-vs-randomness paradigm~\cite{gpw2020}
to reduce general protocols to this case. 

\subsection{Communication lower bound for subcube protocols}
\label{subsec:classical-subcube}
We start by defining the input distribution $\mu$ and its properties. Let $\mu$ be the distribution of independent uniform lists $F_i$ of size $(|\Sigma|-1)/2$. Under $\mu$, we have $\OPT=n$ with probability $1-o(1)$ (See \Cref{hb:random-sat}). Thus, on almost all inputs from $\mu$, an $\alpha$-approximation must satisfy at least $\alpha n$ rows.
In each row, choose a uniform excluded jet $\xi_i$ and a uniform perfect matching of $\Sigma\setminus\{\xi_i\}$, and select one endpoint of every matching edge using an independent fair bit. The resulting list has size
$(|\Sigma|-1)/2$. Its distribution is invariant under all permutations of $\Sigma$, which act transitively on lists of this size; hence it is uniform. Therefore, conditional on the excluded jets and matchings, the orientation bits are uniform and independent, and the rows are sampled
independently. 

Since the hard distribution is satisfiable with probability $1-o(1)$, the protocol outputs a polynomial satisfying at least $\alpha n$ rows with probability at least $2/3-o(1)$. By averaging, we fix the matchings, excluded jets, and random coins while retaining success probability at least $1/2$ over uniform orientation bits. We append a verification step at the end. The output party sends the polynomial's $K$ coefficients, and each party reports the membership bit of the queried jet in each of its rows. We reject invalid polynomials or those satisfying fewer than $\alpha n$ rows. The resulting deterministic protocol is \emph{fully verified}: every accepting transcript determines the output polynomial and all queried membership bits, hence the corresponding orientation bits for nonexcluded jets. This adds only $K\lceil\log q\rceil+n+O(1)=O(\ell nh)$ bits of communication, so it suffices to analyze fully verified protocols. 

\paragraph{Proof for subcube protocols.}
We first define the notion of subcube protocols.
Let $N_a$ be the set of input bits held by party $a$.
A rectangle $R=X_1\times\cdots\times X_k$, where
$X_a\subseteq\{0,1\}^{N_a}$, is a subcube rectangle if each factor $X_a$ fixes some coordinates $I_a\subseteq N_a$ and leaves all other coordinates unrestricted. Its codimension is
$\codim(R)=\sum_a|I_a|$.
A subcube protocol is such that every complete-message node of
the protocol tree corresponds to a subcube rectangle.
This model is closely related to decision trees, but is strictly more general. 

Fix a fully verified subcube protocol $\Pi$ and a matching structure.  For a node $v$, let $F_i(v)$ consist of $\xi_i$ and both endpoints
of every edge whose orientation is fixed by $R_v$. Thus $F_i(v)$ contains addresses with known membership status, whether positive or negative; it is not the input list $F_i$. Since matching edges are disjoint,
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{i\in[n]}|F_i(v)|=n+2\codim(R_v).
 \label{cl:touched}
\end{equation}
Stop the analysis of a path just before its first node with $\sum_{i\in[n]}|F_i(v)|>T$, and call this an abort. For the original random leaf
$R$, uniformity gives $\E\codim(R)=\ent(R)\le|\Pi|$, where $\ent$ is Shannon entropy. By monotonicity and Markov's inequality,
\begin{equation}
 \Pr[\text{abort}]
 \le\frac{2\E[\codim(R)]}{T-n}
 \le\frac{2|\Pi|}{T-n}.
 \label{cl:abort}
\end{equation}

Call a codeword \emph{dangerous} at $v$ if at least
$\lceil\zeta n\rceil$ of its addresses belong to $F(v)$.
For the successive nodes $v_1,\ldots,v_d$ in the stopped path, let
\[
 Q_i=\Lcal_\zeta(F(v_i))
 =\{c\in C_\Hrm:
       |\{r\in[n]:c_r\in F_r(v_i)\}|\ge\lceil\zeta n\rceil\}.
\]
We observe three properties:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*),nosep]
 \item $Q_i\subseteq Q_{i+1}$ for all $i$.
 \item Every successful output on a non-aborting path belongs to $Q_d$.
 \item $|Q_d|\le L$.
\end{enumerate}
Here (i) follows from monotonicity of the fixed coordinates. For (ii), full verification makes every output query's membership status known, so every output address belongs to $F(v_d)$.
For (iii), apply list recovery to the exposed
address sets, rather than to the input lists:
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{r\in[n]}|F_r(v_i)|\le T
 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
 |Q_i|=|\Lcal_\zeta(F(v_i))|\le L.
 \label{cl:list-recovery-use}
\end{equation}
It holds at every node, even though $F(v_i)$ depends on the transcript, because list recovery applies to every family of address sets within the incidence budget. Moreover, (i) gives $\abs{\bigcup_{i=1}^d Q_i}=|Q_d|\le L$. Thus at most $L$ distinct codewords become dangerous along the entire stopped path, not merely at any single node. This is the use of Hermite list recovery in the subcube proof: it bounds the number of candidates in the final union bound. No list-recovery algorithm is run by the protocol.

A codeword dangerous at the root queries at least $\lceil\zeta n\rceil$
excluded jets and cannot satisfy $\alpha n$ rows, since $\zeta>1-\alpha$ for sufficiently large $n$. Order the other dangerous codewords by first appearance, breaking ties by a fixed order, and pad the list with $\bot$ to obtain
$c_1,\ldots,c_L$. Once an index is assigned, its codeword does not change.

\begin{claim}
\label{cl:first-appearance}
For every $j\in[L]$, $\Pr[c_j\ne\bot\text{ and }\err_F(c_j)\le(1-\alpha)n] \le e^{-32n/h^2}$.
\end{claim}
\begin{proof}
Fix $j$ and an entry node $v$ where $c_j$ first becomes dangerous. Before the sender's message, fewer than $\lceil\zeta n\rceil$ of its addresses were exposed. The other parties therefore still hold $t$ unexposed queried bits, where
\begin{equation}
 t\ge n-\lceil n/k\rceil-\lceil\zeta n\rceil
   \ge2(1-\alpha)n+\frac{8n}{h}.
 \label{cl:unfixed-queries}
\end{equation}
Conditional on $R_v$, these bits are independent and fair, possibly complemented. Since $t\le n$, Hoeffding's inequality gives
\[
 \Pr[\err_F(c_j)\le(1-\alpha)n\mid R_v]
 \le\Pr[\operatorname{Bin}(t,1/2)\le(1-\alpha)n]
 \le e^{-32n/h^2}.
\]
The candidate and its index are determined at $v$. For a fixed $j$,
its possible entry nodes form an antichain, so their input events
are disjoint. Averaging the conditional bound over these events proves
the claim. No condition is imposed on the future transcript.
\end{proof}

Every successful non-aborting output is among these at most $L$
candidates. Using the claim and properties (ii)--(iii), we obtain
\begin{align}
 \Pr[\Pi\text{ succeeds and does not abort}]
 &\le\sum_{j=1}^L
   \Pr[c_j\ne\bot\text{ and }\err_F(c_j)\le(1-\alpha)n]
 \notag\\
 &\le L e^{-32n/h^2}
 \notag=\exp\left(O_{\ell,\alpha}(n/h^3)-32n/h^2\right)=o(1).
 \label{cl:subcube}
\end{align}
The list-recovery bound controls the number of candidates, whereas
\Cref{cl:first-appearance} controls the success probability of
each candidate through its still-unexposed queries.
Together with~\eqref{cl:abort}, this gives
\[
 \Pr[\Pi\text{ succeeds}]
 \le\frac{2|\Pi|}{T-n}+o(1).
\]
Therefore, any such protocol with success probability at least
$1/2$ has $|\Pi|=\Omega(T)$.

